WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.949
Congratulations.

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:03.070
You did it.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:07.950
After years of working your fingers to the
bone at the legendary SCP Foundation, putting

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:13.759
your life, sanity, and perhaps even immortal
soul at risk every single day, you suddenly

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:17.370
find yourself standing at the very tip of
the pyramid.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:21.040
You can now learn about the truth behind SCP-001.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.890
You’d be lying if you said it was an easy
journey.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:30.690
You started about one rung above D-Class,
a mere janitor, mopping hallways and cleaning

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:34.520
toilets for personnel who actually got a piece
of the action.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.970
Not that it meant you didn’t have to experience
risk on the job.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.310
You remembered one of the most terrifying
moments of your life occurring during this

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:46.050
period, back when you were just cleaning out
office space in Site-19.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:52.470
You heard the door rattle behind you, and
you rose back up just in time to see SCP-173

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.030
standing in the doorway, staring at you.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.630
You’d heard about this thing, about how
you had to keep your eyes on it, or it’d

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.239
kill you and then everything else.

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:07.440
You stared at it, eyes watering in panic,
hoping to keep it rooted in place.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.080
But it was starting to hurt.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.830
You could feel yourself straining.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.700
The surface of your eyes was so dry.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.770
Was this where you were going to die?

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.820
Perhaps, if you hadn’t had the idea you
had next.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:24.840
You blinked one eye, and then the other, keeping
one open at all times.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.960
That kept the Sculpture in place long enough
for the Site Security Team to locate it, and

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.690
drag it back to containment.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:36.170
You’d saved your own life, and many others
that day, and decided in that moment that

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.530
you may really have a future here.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.710
And you weren’t the only one with this level
of confidence.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.860
Hearing about your quick thinking and heroic
bravery, the head of the site security team

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.689
decided you might be an ideal candidate for
his crew, after the proper training.

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:55.850
You jumped at the chance, of course, to experience
the grueling barrage of physical and mental

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:01.060
courses needed to survive and thrive under
the pressure of being a dedicated Foundation

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.060
guard.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.549
Before long, you became an accepted member
of the security team.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:10.009
And you’d go on to receive further commendations
for your cool-headedness and bravery during

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:18.829
dangerous containment breaches from SCP-3199,
SCP-106, and SCP-939, respectively.

00:02:18.829 --> 00:02:23.510
You were quickly turning into a respected
figure among your Foundation peers.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.409
While some would be content to coast on that,
you’ve never been the kind of person who

00:02:27.409 --> 00:02:29.170
just rests on their laurels.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:35.110
By day, you work to keep Site-19 safe from
deadly containment breaches and infiltration

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.610
from crafty Groups of Interest like the Chaos
Insurgency and the Serpent’s Hand.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:45.439
But by night, you were taking online college
courses, slowly beefing up your qualifications

00:02:45.439 --> 00:02:48.969
in a number of esoteric research sciences.

00:02:48.969 --> 00:02:54.569
After years of hard work, you requested a
transfer from Senior Security Officer to Junior

00:02:54.569 --> 00:02:55.569
Researcher.

00:02:55.569 --> 00:02:59.870
It’d be a step backwards, in terms of career
advancement, and you were also told you’d

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.829
get a pay cut for your trouble.

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:06.209
But you didn’t care; you’d even start
with archival work if it meant getting closer

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:09.590
to the actual anomalies the Foundation was
all about.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:14.529
So that’s exactly what happened: You were
made a junior researcher, and your first job

00:03:14.529 --> 00:03:20.650
was archival duties, sorting and digitizing
years of files with the new computerized system.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.829
For some, this would be hell.

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:24.529
For you, it’s heaven.

00:03:24.529 --> 00:03:31.120
You’re getting to see it all up close: The
deepest, darkest secrets of the SCP Foundation.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.719
Well, not quite.

00:03:32.719 --> 00:03:37.400
At your low clearance level, there’s only
a certain amount you’re able to access,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.420
even when filing things away.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.730
Stumbling into the wrong files and attempting
to access them can be actively dangerous,

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:48.589
too, as many of them are protected with the
kind of heavy-duty memetic kill agents that’d

00:03:48.589 --> 00:03:52.209
stop your heart before you even have a chance
to click away.

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:56.480
But funnily enough, that level of protection
only made the forbidden fruit of knowledge

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.299
seem more enticing.

00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:04.069
And the very peak of curiosities, locked away
under so many layers of secrecy and encryption,

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:09.489
was the equal parts iconic and infamous SCP-001.

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:14.609
Some senior researchers had spread rumors
about the true nature of 001, but nobody truly

00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:18.380
had the full scope of it outside of the O5
Council.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:23.670
For pretty much everyone else, the true answers
would be forever out of reach, like a distant

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.970
but enrapturing mirage.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.480
And it was at that moment that you decided:
You’d simply have to become a member of

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:37.720
the O5 Council, and for the rest of your life,
that’s exactly what you try to work towards.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.760
After all, rumor has it that the Council has
access to secret, life-extending anomalies

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.460
that they use to linger around for centuries.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.750
A single meager lifetime working toward a
seat at the table would be such a small price

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.240
to pay when all was said and done.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:58.220
For the next few decades, you did everything
you could to advance your position and climb

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:01.180
through the ranks of this prestigious organization.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:05.320
You published award-winning research papers
on the nature of Thaumatology, you worked

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:09.440
multiple eighteen-hour shifts a week, getting
up close and personal with some of the most

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:19.600
famous and dangerous anomalies on the books
- SCP-106, SCP-096, and even SCP-682.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.760
You showed bravery and heroism during containment
breaches, staying calm and keeping the people

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.850
around you out of harm’s way.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:31.800
And as you rise through the ranks, you always
keep your goal in mind: You’ll uncover the

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.060
secrets of SCP-001.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.580
You have to know.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:37.860
You will know.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.450
There is no other acceptable outcome.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:47.090
While, technically speaking, 001 is above
classified, the SCP Foundation is an organization

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:52.490
run by humans - Well, mostly, anyway - and
when humans are involved, nothing will ever

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.210
be infallible.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:58.200
And after all, there’s no such thing as
a perfect seal.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.210
When you’re rubbing elbows with Dr. Gears,
Dr. Clef, and Dr. Bright, it’s natural that

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:08.870
some things start to slip out in conversation,
even if, strictly speaking, it’s in defiance

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.390
of official Foundation protocol.

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:16.430
But something strikes you as very strange:
As each one tells you about their vision of

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:22.170
SCP-001, you notice that each is describing
a completely different thing.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:27.430
Dr. Clef, for example, begins covertly telling
you about something known as The Gate Guardian.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:32.140
Also known by the alias Uriel, this being
is said to act as the bouncer at the door

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:33.140
to paradise.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:38.960
He’s impossibly huge, with wings made of
burning light, and a sword of pure fire that’s

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.300
hotter than the sun.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.160
Clef tells you that the creature’s sword
is so powerful that it cleaves its victims

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.380
apart on the atomic level, completely and
utterly destroying every trace of them.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:56.550
Though he then grumbles, with visible frustration,
that it failed in its attempt to kill SCP-682.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.780
Adding “No surprises there, right?”

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:00.390
as he left to have a smoke.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:03.950
Dr. Bright tells you an incredibly different
story.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:09.180
To him, SCP-001 isn’t a being, it’s a
location.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:14.360
An expansive factory from the 19th century
that was created by pure evil occultist and

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.060
industrialist James Anderson.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:21.980
This place initially seemed like a boon, providing
jobs to the needy travelers and transients

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.400
across the early United States.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.889
But this dream come true soon revealed itself
to be a complete nightmare: Anderson was a

00:07:29.889 --> 00:07:35.389
brutal taskmaster, who not only worked thousands
of people to death but performed horrifying

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:38.840
occult experiments on the grounds of the cursed
building.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:42.700
You started to worry that perhaps Dr. Bright
had had a little too much to drink when he

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:47.480
started ranting about how the Factory helped
a burgeoning SCP Foundation commit genocide

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:54.060
against the fae, but you were happy to receive
more information about SCP-001 in any case.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:58.150
And Dr. Gears told a far more lowkey story
than the other two.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:03.040
Which was fitting, really, for a man so incredibly
buttoned-down he probably thought of Lucky

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:07.980
Charm marshmallows as an almost unspeakable
act of self-indulgence.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:13.230
He told the story of a being he referred to
as “The Prototype”, a dangerous and radioactive

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:18.310
monster that functioned as the first truly
threatening creature that the SCP Foundation

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:19.840
ever faced.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:24.810
Containing it with imperfect methods even
led to the death of Dr. Keter, which, in turn,

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:29.340
led to the naming of the Keter Class for any
anomalies that were particularly challenging

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.730
to contain.

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:36.430
You were awash with data, and yet, it somehow
felt like you now knew less for sure.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:41.729
Was SCP-001 some kind of umbrella term for
a vast collection of different anomalies,

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:46.370
each one either deeply important or somehow
formative for the SCP Foundation?

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.889
Curiouser and curiouser.

00:08:48.889 --> 00:08:53.350
Naturally, this did nothing to deter your
boundless drive to know the true secrets of

00:08:53.350 --> 00:09:00.059
this mysterious designation that seemed to
exist at the very heart of the SCP Foundation.

00:09:00.059 --> 00:09:04.260
Years went on, and you went from a decorated
Senior Researcher to the Site Director for

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:05.470
Site 19.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:10.379
There, through even more years of hard work,
you developed a reputation as one of the greatest

00:09:10.379 --> 00:09:12.759
Directors the site ever had.

00:09:12.759 --> 00:09:17.600
You reduced containment breaches by a truly
astonishing eighty percent during your tenure,

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:23.019
and the breaches that did happen were significantly
less deadly under your sterling management.

00:09:23.019 --> 00:09:27.709
General personnel satisfaction skyrocketed,
with the people working under you both enjoying

00:09:27.709 --> 00:09:31.680
your leadership and respecting you enough
to take you seriously.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.440
You were firm but fair.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.040
A truly exemplary site director.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:42.110
The more you climbed, the more information
about SCP-001 seemed to fall into your lap.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:45.240
The sheer breadth of some of these concepts
were staggering.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:51.499
The Scarlet King, The Black Moon, The Lock,
The Broken God, a whole laundry list of terrifying

00:09:51.499 --> 00:09:55.879
existential threats hiding just under the
thin crust of normalcy.

00:09:55.879 --> 00:09:58.029
Just how much were they hiding from you?

00:09:58.029 --> 00:09:59.259
Were they all real?

00:09:59.259 --> 00:10:03.110
Was only one real, and the rest an elaborate
smokescreen?

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:06.970
You just had to know, but even if it took
your whole life?

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:12.029
A 40-year career with the SCP Foundation,
putting everything at hazard for a chance

00:10:12.029 --> 00:10:15.160
to gaze into the ultimate chest of secrets.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:20.360
And eventually, with the mysterious passing
of a previous member of the O5 Council - Which,

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:26.509
as far as anyone else knows, you had nothing
to do with - the position of 05-10 has opened

00:10:26.509 --> 00:10:31.470
up, and who else is next in line to that throne…But
you.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:37.209
Congratulations, you made it to the next step:
the big boys’ table - and the truth about

00:10:37.209 --> 00:10:40.399
SCP-001 comes with the territory.

00:10:40.399 --> 00:10:45.759
You’re given an orientation document written
by a prior O5 Council member known as L.H.

00:10:45.759 --> 00:10:49.259
Sein from the Korean branch of the Foundation.

00:10:49.259 --> 00:10:54.509
It promises that after you put in your new
O5 login credentials, it’ll tell you everything

00:10:54.509 --> 00:10:58.170
you need to know about SCP-001.

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:03.830
With quaking fingers, you type in your username
and password, and the page opens, revealing

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:08.619
the truth about SCP-001 to your eager eyes.

00:11:08.619 --> 00:11:10.050
Your jaw drops.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:13.170
Your forehead is suddenly doused in a sheen
of cold sweat.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:14.920
No, you think.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.050
This has to be a joke.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:17.439
It can’t be true.

00:11:17.439 --> 00:11:21.459
Your life, your whole life, building up to
learning…

00:11:21.459 --> 00:11:25.329
SCP-001 does not exist.

00:11:25.329 --> 00:11:30.779
The document, in upsettingly plain terms,
explains to you that while some of the anomalies

00:11:30.779 --> 00:11:38.240
referred to as SCP-001 do exist in some capacity
- the closest, funnily enough, being Dr. Gears’

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:43.930
story about The Prototype - for the most part,
there is no such thing as 001, and the finer

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:50.559
details are either entirely fabricated or,
at the very least, greatly exaggerated.

00:11:50.559 --> 00:11:54.839
Everything you’ve been working towards,
it was just one big fraud perpetrated by the

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:55.839
Foundation.

00:11:55.839 --> 00:11:56.839
Why?

00:11:56.839 --> 00:11:59.080
You found yourself asking in a trembling voice.

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:00.839
Why, why, why?

00:12:00.839 --> 00:12:06.050
And the answer was simple: To provoke the
egos of the Foundation’s personnel.

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:11.759
You see, while the Foundation has always contained
anomalies, that was a means to an end, not

00:12:11.759 --> 00:12:13.589
the end itself.

00:12:13.589 --> 00:12:18.309
The true purpose of the Foundation is and
always has been protecting people, and every

00:12:18.309 --> 00:12:21.240
anomaly contained is more people protected.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.830
However, tricking the lower rungs of the Foundation
into believing that there’s some grand Final

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:31.459
Boss to work towards - like a Black Moon or
a Scarlet King - has been an effective method

00:12:31.459 --> 00:12:35.709
of keeping everyone motivated to swim in the
same direction.

00:12:35.709 --> 00:12:39.889
This is hardly an unprecedented move for the
SCP Foundation.

00:12:39.889 --> 00:12:44.949
From their lies around Procedure 110 Montauk
to the controlled deception around the rituals

00:12:44.949 --> 00:12:48.809
that supposedly keep The Devourer of Worlds
on the other side of SCP-2317.

00:12:48.809 --> 00:12:55.920
The SCP Foundation has never shied away from
lying to its own employees for the greater

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:01.449
good, but you never expected it to happen
to you, and you certainly never expected it

00:13:01.449 --> 00:13:04.379
to be about SCP-001.

00:13:04.379 --> 00:13:09.470
You feel like you’ve been laughed at for
your entire life, but now you crack a quivering

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:10.470
smile.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:16.819
Maybe your mind has snapped, or maybe, you’re
just glad to finally be in on the joke.

00:13:16.819 --> 00:13:23.259
Now go check out “SCP-001 - The World's
Gone Beautiful” and “SCP-001 Ouroboros

00:13:23.259 --> 00:13:28.769
Cycle - The Full Story Compilation” for
more strange and original takes on SCP-001!

